## 2026-09-04 (solar-time window correction)

- User proposed adopting HECO's 10am-2pm minimum-daytime-load window on physical grounds (solar
  irradiance peaks reliably around solar noon), computed from the appropriate UTC offset rather than a
  literal local-clock reading. Worked through the precise translation rather than adopt the number as
  stated: found the dominant correction is not Virginia's small longitude offset from the Eastern Time
  meridian (~10 min for Richmond) but Daylight Saving Time itself (a full hour, in effect for the
  higher-solar-output ~8 months of the year). A literal "hour 10-14" window against locally-timestamped
  data would miss true solar noon by a full hour for most of the year. Adopted a UTC-anchored window
  (15:00-19:00 UTC year-round) instead, translating to 10am-2pm EST in winter months but 11am-3pm EDT
  during DST months. Flagged as a standing implementation point for any future minimum-daytime-load
  work, not specific to this one item.

