## 2026-09-05 (documentation + 1-day PJM-aligned lookahead)

- Documented the full 6-day lookahead run (Six_Day_Lookahead_Firming_Documentation.md): citation,
  algorithm explanation, formula, and results. Caught and corrected a real citation problem before
  writing this rather than after: fetched the actual MDPI paper directly (had only received a pasted
  generic explainer alongside the URL two turns ago, never the paper itself) and found it is a
  machine-learning FORECASTING paper (XGBoost/CatBoost predicting future output), not a battery-dispatch/
  firming-optimization method -- fundamentally different from what this project built (deterministic,
  perfect-foresight retrospective optimization). Cited the paper accurately for what it actually
  contributes (real support that 6-day NWP forecast skill is legitimate) rather than mis-cite it as the
  source of this project's own different methodology.
- Ran the same two configurations (39 deg latitude-tilt rooftop vs. 45 deg rooftop, both with 15 deg
  canopy, true 4-county 6,159.0 MW fleet, Sterling+Arlington insolation) at a 1-day (24hr) lookahead,
  per direct user reasoning (alignment with PJM's own day-ahead market clearing cycle).
- REAL, VERIFIED FINDING, not an edge-case artifact -- checked the full 21-point curve before trusting
  the "best" result, given "0% short-duration, 0.00 MW" looked suspiciously different from every prior
  finding (all of which found 100% short-duration optimal): every single split point (0% to 100%) returns
  EXACTLY 0.00 MW worst-day firm level, for BOTH tilt configurations. Root cause, physically sound: with
  only 24 hours of foresight, the algorithm cannot pre-position battery charge ahead of any single
  genuinely severe (near-zero-solar) day it can't yet see coming when that day's own dispatch decision is
  made -- since the metric is the MINIMUM across all 3,277 real days, one such day anywhere in the 9-year
  record drags the entire worst-case floor to zero, regardless of storage duration split (neither short
  nor long duration has any foresight advantage at this horizon). This is a direct, concrete demonstration
  of why lookahead window length matters for this reliability metric -- the 3-day and 6-day windows
  produced meaningful positive floors specifically because they let the algorithm see and pre-position for
  approaching low-solar stretches; a 1-day window structurally cannot.

